keystone sm: register the AMOEBA CVW platform override

OpenSBI picks a platform_override module by matching the device tree root
node's compatible string.  Keystone's generic override lists only QEMU's
"riscv-virtio" and "riscv-virtio,qemu", so on our board the module is never
selected, generic_final_init() never runs, and sm_init() is never called.

The failure is silent and looks like success: the SM object code links into
the firmware, OpenSBI boots normally, Linux boots normally, and the only
symptom is that no [SM] line ever appears on the console.  Verified before
this patch -- fw_payload.elf contained sbi_sm_create_enclave, attest_enclave
and the sanctum key region, yet the SM never initialised.

Adds "amoeba,cvw", matching the root compatible in
testcode/linux/dts/amoeba.dts.

--- a/sm/plat/generic/generic.c
+++ b/sm/plat/generic/generic.c
@@ -13,6 +13,15 @@
 static const struct fdt_match generic_match[] = {
 	{ .compatible = "riscv-virtio" },
 	{ .compatible = "riscv-virtio,qemu" },
+	/*
+	 * AMOEBA CVW SoC.  OpenSBI selects a platform_override module by
+	 * matching the root node's compatible string, so without an entry here
+	 * generic_final_init() is never called, sm_init() never runs, and the
+	 * SM is silently linked-but-dead: the firmware boots, Linux boots, and
+	 * nothing reports an error.  Keystone upstream only ever targeted QEMU
+	 * and real boards, so this list has no wildcard.
+	 */
+	{ .compatible = "amoeba,cvw" },
 	{ },
 };
 
